Q1. What is the difference between a Docker Image and a Docker Container?

Interview Ready Answer

A Docker image is an immutable, read-only template that contains the
application, dependencies, libraries, runtime, and configuration required
to run an application.

A Docker container is a running instance of a Docker image. It adds a
writable layer on top of the image and executes the application in an
isolated environment.

Definition

Docker Image
- Read-only template
- Contains application + dependencies
- Created using a Dockerfile and docker build

Docker Container
- Running instance of an image
- Has its own writable layer
- Executes the application

Purpose

• Image stores application and dependencies.
• Container runs the application.

======================================================================

Q2. What is the purpose of the docker run command?

Interview Ready Answer

The docker run command creates a new container from a Docker image and
starts it. If the image is not available locally, Docker first downloads
it from the configured container registry and then creates and starts the
container.

Internal Working

docker run
     │
     ▼
Check image locally
     │
     ├── Found → Use image
     │
     └── Not Found
             │
             ▼
        Pull image
             │
             ▼
Create new container
             │
             ▼
Add writable layer
             │
             ▼
Configure network/ports/volumes
             │
             ▼
Start container
             │
             ▼
Execute ENTRYPOINT/CMD

Purpose

• Creates a new container.
• Starts the container.
• Pulls image automatically if required.

======================================================================

Q3. What is the purpose of the docker ps command?

Interview Ready Answer

The docker ps command lists all currently running Docker containers.

Purpose

• View running containers.
• Verify whether a container is running.

======================================================================

Q4. What is the difference between docker ps and docker ps -a?

Interview Ready Answer

docker ps displays only running containers.

docker ps -a displays all containers, including running, stopped, exited,
and newly created containers.

Purpose

• docker ps → Running containers only.
• docker ps -a → All containers.

======================================================================

Q5. What is the purpose of the docker stop command?

Interview Ready Answer

The docker stop command gracefully stops a running container. Docker first
sends a SIGTERM signal to allow the application to shut down cleanly. If
the application does not stop within the timeout period, Docker sends
SIGKILL to terminate it forcefully.

Purpose

• Gracefully stop a running container.
• Prevent abrupt application termination.

======================================================================

Q6. What is the difference between docker stop and docker kill?

Interview Ready Answer

docker stop performs a graceful shutdown by sending SIGTERM first and
SIGKILL only if required.

docker kill immediately sends SIGKILL (or another specified signal) and
terminates the container without waiting.

Purpose

• docker stop → Graceful shutdown.
• docker kill → Immediate termination.

======================================================================

Q7. What is the purpose of the docker rm command?

Interview Ready Answer

The docker rm command removes a Docker container. By default, it removes
only stopped containers. A running container must be stopped first or
removed forcefully using docker rm -f.

Purpose

• Remove Docker containers.
• Clean up unused containers.

======================================================================

Q8. What is the difference between docker rm and docker rmi?

Interview Ready Answer

docker rm removes Docker containers.

docker rmi removes Docker images.

A Docker image cannot be removed while it is being used by an existing
container.

Purpose

• docker rm → Remove containers.
• docker rmi → Remove images.

======================================================================

Q9. What is the purpose of the docker images command?

Interview Ready Answer

The docker images command lists all Docker images available on the local
Docker host. It displays details such as repository, tag, image ID,
creation time, and image size.

Purpose

• View locally available images.

======================================================================

Q10. What is the purpose of the docker pull command?

Interview Ready Answer

The docker pull command downloads a Docker image from a container
registry (such as Docker Hub) to the local Docker host.

Purpose

• Download images from a registry.
• Fetch required images before running containers.

======================================================================

Q11. What is the purpose of the docker exec command?

Interview Ready Answer

The docker exec command executes a command inside an already running
Docker container. It is commonly used for debugging, inspection, and
administration of running containers.

Purpose

• Execute commands inside a running container.
• Debug or inspect running applications.

======================================================================

Q12. What is the difference between docker run and docker exec?

Interview Ready Answer

docker run creates a new container from a Docker image and starts it.

docker exec executes a command inside an already running container without
creating a new container.

Purpose

• docker run → Create and start a new container.
• docker exec → Execute commands in an existing running container.

